    "body": "Carla Babb is joining Military Times and Defense News as our new Head of Content. In nearly two decades covering national security and international affairs, Babb has earned a reputation for fearless, fact-driven journalism and deep relationships across the U.S. defense establishment.\nRecognized as one of the nation’s most candid and well-sourced defense journalists, Babb has broken major stories, pressed senior military leaders for answers and reported from conflict zones around the world. In her new role, she will spearhead editorial initiatives, lead enterprise projects and expand the publications’ presence at major events while developing new ways to engage military, defense industry and national security audiences.\nBabb will work alongside Editor-in-Chief J.D. Simkins, who continues to lead coverage by the Military Times and Defense News newsroom. Under Simkins, the newsroom has published its 10 most-read stories of all time within the last year. Simkins was also a recent recipient of the Timothy White Award for Editorial Integrity at SIIA’s Jesse H. Neal Awards. Together, they will lead the next chapter for the brands.\n“Carla is one of the most respected and versatile journalists covering national security, and she is the right person to help lead what comes next for Military Times and Defense News,” said Kelly Facer, Senior Vice President at Sightline Media Group, which publishes Military Times and Defense News. “For decades, the military and defense community has trusted these brands to tell them what is really happening. With Carla and J.D. leading our editorial work together, we will build on that trust as we expand into new coverage areas, programs and events for our audience.”\nBabb most recently served as national security correspondent for Newsmax and spent more than a decade as Voice of America’s Pentagon correspondent, reaching a global audience of hundreds of millions. She has also reported for Military Times and Defense News.\nBabb’s scoops include the 2020 Iranian missile attack on U.S. troops at Iraq’s Al-Asad Air Base, U.S. military interdictions of illicit oil tankers in 2025, and troop deployments and military operations during the 2026 war with Iran. Her interviews with senior military leaders have generated headlines across major national outlets, including The Washington Post, Newsweek, Fox News and NBC News.\n“Military Times and Defense News are where service members and their families turn for unvarnished insight and accountability on the issues that shape their lives, and I’m excited about where we can take them next,” said Babb. “I’m eager to get to work with Jon and this newsroom to chase the stories our readers need, widen our coverage and shape national security conversations.”\nA prominent advocate for independent defense journalism, Babb serves as vice president of the board of Military Reporters and Editors.\nLater this week, Babb will receive the prestigious Prize of Excellence from the Association of Foreign Correspondents in the United States for her outstanding contributions to journalism and distinguished national security reporting. Her work has also earned recognition from the Gerald R. Ford Presidential Foundation, the Press Club of Atlantic City, and regional Associated Press and Society of Professional Journalists chapters.",
